# DESCRIPTION

cdns-i2c drives a Cadence I2C controller (r1p14) of the CIX Sky1 and serves its bus to the devices on it.
devmgr(8) starts one for each controller that matches its record (driver(6)):

```text
driver cdns-i2c
class i2c
match acpi CIXH200B
```

The Sky1 has eight, `\_SB_.I2C0` to `I2C7`, each with 64 KiB of registers and one interrupt.

The driver turns its power resource on (`/dev/acpi/power`), turns its clock on through scmi(3) by the id its CLKT names (`/dev/acpi/clocks`) and reads that clock's rate with `clock <id> rate`.
The rate is the reference of the bus's bit rate, which the controller makes by two dividers (SCL is the input rate over 22 x (A + 1) x (B + 1)).
It runs the bus at the device's `clock-frequency` (`/dev/acpi/properties`) at most 400 kHz, else 100 kHz, at the nearest rate the dividers make that doesn't go over it; a bus that can't be made at that rate is run at 100 kHz.
Where scmi(3) gives no rate, the driver assumes 100 MHz and says so in the status.
The controller's reset is taken as firmware left it.

A transfer is made as messages: the bytes written, then after a repeated START the bytes read, one STOP at the end.
Each is made through the controller's 16-byte FIFOs and polled, with interrupts masked.
A message longer than the FIFO, or one that another follows, holds the bus (HOLD) until its last bytes are queued or expected.
A read longer than the counter's chunk (the width of its transfer-size register as probed, less three) keeps the counter ahead of the bus by writing the next chunk when the FIFO is full.
A transfer with nothing to write and nothing to read, a probe, reads one byte and drops it.
Each wait is for up to 100 ms, and the bus is waited for up to 500 ms to be quiet before a transfer.
The controller is reset after one that fails.
Transfers are made one at a time, in the order they come.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices on its bus.

: `bus`
  The bus, as vectraos.i2c's `Bus`: `transfer(address, write, read)` sends `write` to the 7-bit address and then reads `read` bytes (either may be empty; both empty is a probe), at most 256 each, and returns what it read.
  It fails with `noDevice` where nothing acknowledges the address (a NACK before any byte moved), `nak` where a byte isn't acknowledged, `busError` (a FIFO overflow or underflow, a message that ended short, or a bus that stayed busy), `arbitrationLost`, `timedOut`, or `tooLong`.
: `status`
  Read only.
  How it was brought up (the power resource, the clock and its rate, what the counter read back and the chunk it makes); `NOT RESPONDING` and the registers where TIME_OUT, CR or the counter didn't read back what was written; the bus's rate in kHz; `transfers <n>, failed <n>`; and `last failure: 0x<address>: <why>` with the controller's ISR, SR and counter, or CR, SR, ISR and counter for a timeout.

