# BUGS

The controller's reset is taken as firmware left it: a controller firmware left in reset gives registers that don't read back what was written, which the status says (`NOT RESPONDING`), and every transfer then times out.
The driver has no interrupts: ISR is polled.
The input clock's rate is read from the SCMI agent; where it can't be, the divider is made from an assumed 100 MHz and the bus may run at the wrong rate.
A NACK is told as no device before any byte moved and as a byte refused after, from the FIFO's count, which isn't known to hold for a write after its address was refused.
A probe reads one byte, which a device may take as a read.
Ten-bit addresses, SMBus block reads and the controller as a target aren't supported.
A bus a target holds low isn't recovered by clocking it free.
